  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "$\\frac{4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{{sec}^{2}\u2061x-1}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{{sec}^{2}\u2061x-1} & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{{sec}^{2}\u2061x-1}=\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}.\n$$",
